WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.733
Earth had nuclear reactors

2
00:00:01.733 --> 00:00:03.867
about two billion years ago.

3
00:00:03.867 --> 00:00:05.167
Nobody built them.

4
00:00:05.533 --> 00:00:06.500
At Oklo,

5
00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:08.933
in what is now Gabon,

6
00:00:08.933 --> 00:00:10.400
uranium-rich rock sustained

7
00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:11.867
nuclear chain reactions.

8
00:00:12.067 --> 00:00:15.700
Scientists found the clues
in 1972:

9
00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:16.900
missing uranium-235,

10
00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:20.533
and the distinctive
products of fission.

11
00:00:20.733 --> 00:00:22.433
But radioactive rock alone

12
00:00:22.433 --> 00:00:24.133
is not a reactor.

13
00:00:24.133 --> 00:00:25.433
One splitting nucleus

14
00:00:25.433 --> 00:00:26.700
must release neutrons

15
00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:28.833
that can split more nuclei,

16
00:00:28.833 --> 00:00:30.533
keeping a chain going.

17
00:00:30.733 --> 00:00:33.833
Back then, natural uranium

18
00:00:33.833 --> 00:00:36.133
contained more uranium-235,

19
00:00:36.133 --> 00:00:38.433
because that isotope

20
00:00:38.433 --> 00:00:41.533
decays faster
than uranium-238.

21
00:00:41.733 --> 00:00:43.633
Geology concentrated
the uranium.

22
00:00:43.633 --> 00:00:46.033
The deposit had to retain

23
00:00:46.033 --> 00:00:46.967
enough neutrons,

24
00:00:46.967 --> 00:00:48.400
with few impurities

25
00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:50.300
that would absorb them.

26
00:00:50.667 --> 00:00:52.000
Groundwater supplied

27
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:53.967
another crucial ingredient.

28
00:00:54.167 --> 00:00:55.767
Collisions with hydrogen

29
00:00:55.767 --> 00:00:57.333
in the water

30
00:00:57.333 --> 00:00:58.933
slowed the neutrons,

31
00:00:58.933 --> 00:01:01.067
making further
uranium-235 fissions

32
00:01:01.067 --> 00:01:02.133
more likely.

33
00:01:02.133 --> 00:01:04.767
Enough kept the chain alive.

34
00:01:04.967 --> 00:01:07.267
In one studied
reaction zone,

35
00:01:07.267 --> 00:01:08.667
xenon isotope evidence

36
00:01:08.667 --> 00:01:10.033
points to repeated

37
00:01:10.033 --> 00:01:11.433
starts and stops.

38
00:01:11.833 --> 00:01:12.733
Researchers infer

39
00:01:12.733 --> 00:01:14.100
that heating drove

40
00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:15.467
moderating water away,

41
00:01:15.467 --> 00:01:16.833
stopping the chain.

42
00:01:16.833 --> 00:01:19.067
Cooling let water return,

43
00:01:19.067 --> 00:01:20.767
and fission restart.

44
00:01:20.967 --> 00:01:22.600
That cycle is reconstructed

45
00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:24.633
from traces in the rock,

46
00:01:24.633 --> 00:01:26.267
not an ancient clock

47
00:01:26.267 --> 00:01:27.067
anyone watched.

48
00:01:27.267 --> 00:01:28.300
No machinery.

49
00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:29.367
No electricity.

50
00:01:29.367 --> 00:01:31.967
Just heat from
nuclear fission,

51
00:01:31.967 --> 00:01:33.533
sustained by fuel,

52
00:01:33.533 --> 00:01:35.100
water, and geology,

53
00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:37.200
long before humans existed.
